plan Verification
title   Verification
status  archived
goal
    Deliver evidence-backed verification and dependency-ordered implementation for the
    greenfield Flow State rewrite in packages/flow-state-rewrite. Workflow Plans own
    migrated semantics; source contracts supply provenance and trace IDs only. This Plan
    owns proof routing, test coverage, review gates, and promotion evidence. The rewrite
    is incomplete; no Plan claim means implementation or product cutover is complete.

outcomes
    O1  Contract-ordered implementation
        Each implementation slice names its owning Plan clauses, one cohesive module and
        its production owner. It records acceptance behavior, proof IDs, dependencies,
        non-goals, deleted-surface absence and a focused check before coding. The six
        dependency phases remain in order.
    O2  Production-owner proof coverage
        Each active invariant has one decisive executable proof through its
        production owner. Integration gates add reachability or a distinct failure
        mode without copying the owner's complete semantic matrix.
    O3  Story and model behavior
        The shared production Runtime demonstrates accepted Story construction,
        commands, clocks, checkpoints, failure evidence, pure model discovery and
        live parity.
    O4  Evidence-backed closure
        Required proof and integration gates are run and recorded before any
        replacement-promotion claim. No Plan entry itself reports a passed gate.

decisions
    D1  Workflow authority and implementation order
        Workflow Plans own migrated semantics for the greenfield rewrite. Source
        contracts and proof matrices retain trace IDs and migration evidence only;
        they do not override Plan semantics. Archived revisions, proposals, audits,
        research, the reference package, and historical fixtures are provenance only.
        This authority covers rewrite requirements, not product documentation or
        task status. Workflow Tasks own current status; Beads records are historical
        snapshots, not readiness or semantic authority. It claims no implementation,
        proof completion, package promotion, or product cutover;
        @plan.Cutover.O3 and @plan.Cutover.V7 remain
        pending. Build in packages/flow-state-rewrite; packages/flow-state remains
        migration evidence until late cutover. Check copied legacy paths against their
        current owner, types, failure lanes, cleanup, and tests. Do not infer semantics
        or add compatibility from historical material.

        Implement in this dependency order:
        1. Static foundation: identity, routes, types, definitions, operation
        types, and pure app/module compilation. See @V1.
        2. Runtime ownership: readiness, closed admission, leases, mailboxes,
        context, scheduling, lifecycle, and cleanup. See @V2.
        3. Operation kernels: canonical keys, resource stores, generations,
        transactions, streams, overlays, cancellation, invalidation and fanout.
        See @V3.
        4. Persistence and evidence: declarations, capture/hydration, bounded
        codecs, inspection, artifacts and diagnostics. See @V4, @V5, and @V6.
        5. Hosts, Stories and CLI: React attachment, fixtures, seeds, Story
        execution, gateways and the shared CLI executor. See @V4, @V5, and @V6.
        6. Cutover and absence: entrypoints, packed consumers, browser behavior,
        deletion proofs and final package cutover. See @plan.Cutover.V1 through
        @plan.Cutover.V7 and @V7 here.

        The workflow dependency table is a decomposition boundary, not a second
        semantic specification. Splitting a phase is valid only when each slice
        preserves the exact Plan references, source trace IDs, proof owner, acceptance behavior,
        dependencies and non-goals. @plan.Runtime.O2 records the canonical
        identity separation; @plan.Runtime.O3 and @plan.Runtime.O5
        own the related actor and snapshot semantics.
    D2  One proof owner and proportional integration
        A test names its unique invariant or failure. Use one to three decisive tests
        per slice by default; add more only for distinct lanes, races, public type
        cases or required boundary vectors. One test may satisfy several proof IDs
        when it exercises the same invariant through the same production owner.
        Focused production-owner tests prove semantics. Packed consumers, browser
        tests, Stories and installed-CLI tests prove public reachability and
        integration. Repeat semantic vectors only for distinct integration failures,
        races, cleanup or wire boundaries. Source scans, typechecks, generated output
        and diff checks do not prove runtime behavior.
    D3  Cohesive module and static route ownership
        Each cohesive feature owns one top-level module folder. Keep small features
        flat; use a recursive feature folder only for a major self-contained module.
        Co-locate public types, implementation, private helpers/codecs and local
        tests. Folder names describe features, not model/type/API proof roles.
        Re-export files exist only for a real public route; src/public and package
        entrypoints are facades.

        Static Implementation route files are package.json, src/index.ts,
        src/public/root.ts and src/public/types.ts, with the route proof in @V1.
        Provider graph
        construction belongs in a cohesive src/implementation feature module with
        local types, helpers and tests. The route only re-exports that owner; static
        construction is inert and does not acquire Runtime, Scope or readiness.
        The root runtime value has exactly succeed, effect and merge plus its
        same-name public type. Provider construction is separate from Runtime
        acquisition and belongs to the static owner in @V1.
        Focused route checks are nub run --filter flow-state-rewrite check:types and
        nubx vp test packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/implementation/test/public-routes.test.ts.
    D4  Single production ownership
        Live hosts, Stories, tests, SSR and CLI share one production Runtime and
        assembly boundary. AppPlan is the closed-world admission boundary. Production
        owners hold Runtime, actor, operation, persistence, artifact and evidence
        state; adapters supply inputs only. Models remain pure, and snapshot reads
        remain synchronous. Stories receive complete typed Implementations and
        Fixtures. Fix configuration, identity, providers, clocks, seeds, fixtures and
        persistence before Runtime construction.

        Reject dynamic registration, process-global registries and secondary
        runtimes, actors, mailboxes and schedulers. Reject duplicate stores, kernels,
        caches, snapshots, cleanup or evidence systems. Also reject runtime-backed
        models, Effect interpretation in reads, Story interception/result injection,
        parallel codecs/diagnostics and post-materialization mutation. Keep runtime
        semantics at their owning Plan.
    D5  Portable text and bytes
        Shared definition, persistence, artifact, stable-ref and CLI codecs use
        host-neutral ECMAScript/Web APIs on Node, Bun, Deno and browser targets.
        Use WHATWG TextEncoder bytes and byte counts. Reject lone
        surrogates before encoding with String.prototype.isWellFormed or an
        equivalent fallback. Shared package code does not use Node Buffer,
        runtime-specific encoders, UTF-16 length for byte limits, silent surrogate
        replacement, or a second handwritten encoder.

        - Share one UTF-8 helper across definition, persistence, artifact, stable-ref
          and CLI owners.
        - Run full vectors on pinned Node: ASCII, 2/3/4-byte UTF-8, 256/257-byte
          names, lone surrogates and composed/decomposed spelling.
        - Run the compiled codec on Bun, Deno, Chromium, Gecko and WebKit. Check
          ASCII, multibyte UTF-8, lone-surrogate rejection and canonical artifact
          bytes or digest. Record exact host versions.
        - Keep focused tests for host adapters. Equal accepted inputs produce equal
          bytes and length-prefixed identities. Invalid durable input fails before
          serialization or activation.
    D6  Review and decisive test gates
        - Review every diff, owning Plan clause, trace-only provenance and proof
          obligation. Run focused checks and `nub run lint`; fix changed-slice
          formatting and lint defects.
        - Review success, typed failure, defects, interruption, ownership, cleanup,
          ordering and boundaries through production owners. Check duplicate
          authority, hidden compatibility, casts, uncontrolled async work, partial
          updates, bespoke Effect clones and implementation-only tests.
        - Each ready slice names exact clauses, a bounded module, allowed files,
          owner and public/internal output. It records Effect A/E/R, acceptance,
          proof IDs, command, dependencies, non-goals, deleted-surface absence and
          handback.
          Return unknown public names, carriers, constructors, inspection routes or
          errors to the owning Plan. Infer a shape only when a helper alias would
          create another authority.
        - Record contract IDs, changed files, focused checks and `nub run lint`
          pass/fail. Also record reviewed behavior, fixed findings, blocking findings
          and remaining advisory findings. No slice advances with a blocking finding.
        - Prove each invariant through its production owner. Prioritize ownership,
          ordering, atomicity, cleanup, failure, interruption, hostile wire/artifact
          input, malformed authoring input, exact typing and deletions. Use the
          production Runtime, TestClock or Flow testing time, bounded
          Deferred/Queue/PubSub controls and minimal fixtures. Source-only tests do
          not prove races, cleanup, runtime behavior or artifacts.
    D7  Proof-plan ownership
        This Plan owns the cross-cutting proof catalogue, sequencing and shared
        gates. Cutover owns the deletion/retention ledger and no-residue
        scenarios; refer to @plan.Cutover.O2 and @plan.Cutover.O3
        rather than restating its cutover semantics.

constraints
    C1  Authority, package and evidence status
        Workflow Plans own migrated semantics; source clauses are provenance and
        trace labels. The greenfield package is packages/flow-state-rewrite; a green
        check in frozen packages/flow-state is migration evidence, not replacement
        proof. The rewrite is incomplete, so proof, promotion, documentation, and
        product cutover outcomes remain pending until their own owners record them.
    C2  Diagnostic ownership
        @plan.Diagnostics.O1 owns the sole typed Diagnostic model. It owns stable
        code/path/details, evolving summary/help and
        machine-configuration reasons. It translates through Schema, Result and
        Effect, renders safely, supports synchronous FlowUsageError and preserves
        complete Cause/Exit. Create no second schema; let no expected generic Error
        escape a Flow boundary.

        Canonical diagnostic proofs check:
        - Stable Schema identifier and Diagnostic tag, `instanceof Error`, and a
          Schema round-trip to a Diagnostic instance.
        - Direct Result failure and Effect yielding; compact/pretty rendering with
          escaped paths and sorted details.
        - Absence of an `InvalidMachineConfiguration` Error carrier.

        Pure definition/machine Results use Result<A, Diagnostic>; authored callback
        exceptions remain defects.
    C3  Scope of proof matrices
        PROOF rows and local proof IDs are requirements, not task-closure units.
        Retired Phase 0 IDs/receipts are historical evidence only. Current
        executable proofs are owned by production code/tests named by the
        implementation task and active matrix. No full semantic matrix is copied
        into each consumer gate.
    C4  Host and boundary proofs
        Host-version receipts are part of the portable-codec evidence. Keep the full
        vector set with the Node owner and compact portability vectors on other
        hosts. Do not turn implementation internals or unobservable micro-order
        into acceptance criteria.
    C5  Promotion
        A slice is ready for dependents only when blocking review findings are
        cleared, required focused checks pass, and decisive owner proof is recorded.
        Complete the replacement only after every final gate passes. Gates cover
        public behavior/types, failure lanes, cleanup, persistence, artifacts, CLI,
        React hosts, Stories, packed consumers, browser behavior and deletion proofs.
        Cutover requires the full proof set; one focused test, typecheck, lint run or
        source scan is insufficient. Promotion cannot add compatibility behavior or
        semantics without an owning Plan change.
    C6  Source traceability
        Migration sources and trace IDs:
        - reference/incident-console/implementation/contracts/TESTING.md
        - reference/incident-console/implementation/contracts/PROOF_MATRIX.md
        - reference/incident-console/implementation/contracts/IMPLEMENTATION_WORKFLOW.md
        CONTRACT.md §§8, 11 and 12 remain unaccepted examples, checklist and
        provenance. These source files do not override mapped Plan semantics.

verification
    V1  Static types and composition
        Establishes @O1 and @O2.

        - Compile small, medium and large fixtures with the pinned compiler. Cover
          exact definitions, inherited context/bindings, recursive state/configuration
          literals, modules, closed App.M, O/P/K, refs, leases and hooks. Also cover
          closed Story options/targets, observations, checkpoint-name accumulation,
          run-key inference and machine/event/selected/resource/transaction/stream/
          input/context/memory/lifecycle domains.
        - Preserve accepted literals. Reject unknown tokens, missing/extra compound
          nodes, wrong-machine events, missing input/context, extra fields and invalid
          bindings/targets. Also reject duplicate or widened checkpoint names,
          unknown keys, P/K misuse, custom comparators and deleted imports.
        - Preserve exact selected-value, previous-value and nullable-domain types.
          Initial results expose no previous value.
        - Runtime previous/current values, selector equality and named-record
          suppression belong to @V2, @V3 and @V5.

        V1 proves compile-time shapes only. Actor/runtime behavior belongs to @V2;
        operation behavior to @V3; Stories to @V4; React/inspection to @V5;
        persistence/CLI to @V6; deletion proofs to @plan.Cutover.V3 and @V5.
        Static source traces are PROOF-001 and TYPE-P01–P04 in PROOF_MATRIX.md.
        Their target semantics belong to @plan.Types.O1/O2, @plan.PublicSurface.O2/O3,
        @plan.Runtime.O1/O2, and @plan.Diagnostics.O1.

        The @V1 compact typing illustration remains:
        ```ts
        App.M;
        P; // complete executable input
        K; // canonical operation identity
        actorRef; // stable identity
        ```

        Check: nubx tsc -p packages/flow-state-rewrite/tsconfig.json --noEmit.
        Focused executable type-proof path is assigned by implementation work and is
        pending until named; compiler errors block.
    V2  Runtime ownership, turns and lifetime
        Establishes @O1 and @O2.

        - Exercise production mailbox acknowledgement, reentrancy and macrostep
          planning. Validate the full batch before publication. Acknowledge after
          the ordinary turn stabilizes and its evidence is accepted. Prove atomic
          actor/store publication, context bootstrap and current/previous values.
        - Prove runtime defaults/reentry, malformed-name and depth rejection,
          AppPlan admission, and bootstrap rollback through @plan.Runtime.V1–V3.
        - Keep `actor.send` synchronous. Reject transition-direct calls and unrelated
          drains. Projection-only publication skips transition, guard, action,
          redirect, memory, finite-work and hidden-event evaluation. Work starts or
          joins only after publication.
        - Enter mailboxes after initiating publication. Suppress stale/unrelated
          revisions. Coalesce only adjacent projection-only facts.
        - Use one Runtime for live hosts and Stories. Prove explicit owner leases,
          restored/stable ownership, concurrent idempotent disposal, shutdown,
          dependent rejection, tombstones, provider closure and cleanup rollback.
        - Check persistence membership, restore-before-subscribe, ordered/coalesced
          writes, stale-write fencing, fresh Story providers and final flush. Cover
          defaults, descriptor opt-in, exclusion-only filters, JSON rejection,
          custom codecs and exclusion of local/disposed/tombstoned actors.

        The @V2 ownership illustration is:
        ```ts
        actor.send(event): void
        runtime.ensureActor
        runtime.createActor
        ```

        Check: focused production-owner tests for mailbox, lifecycle, cleanup and
        parity. Exact paths remain pending in the implementation proof record;
        do not substitute source scans. See @plan.Runtime.O3 for actor
        identity/lease/admission semantics.
    V3  Operation identity, stores and effects
        Establishes @O1 and @O2.

        - Prove descriptor-plus-canonical-K identity, complete executable P
          retention, bounded portable UTF-8, record-order equivalence, key(-0) as
          canonical 0, and defensive copy/freeze. Equal owners keep pinned P;
          explicit refetch replaces it. Hydration retains key-only data until live P.
        - Prove deterministic binding choice, one Runtime-scoped store, same-runtime
          sharing and cross-runtime/SSR/test/browser isolation. Check exact family
          methods and P/K, passive reads, ownership, cancellation, adapter
          point-of-no-return, selector previous/current values, Object.is equality,
          and independent named-record suppression. @plan.Operations.O3 owns onMemory
          activation/reentry with previous undefined, then the immediately preceding
          value on later changes.
        - Prove generation fencing, setData, explicit updater decline and owner-only
          preview visibility. Prove promotion/rollback, post-boundary unknown,
          canonical-base CAS and actor conflict evidence.
        - Reject invalid identity before ownership, mutation, admission or work.
          Prevent late result/finalizer mutation and cross-actor preview visibility.
        - Prove equal normalized retention and replacement on field, kind, descriptor
          or key change. Release on false/null/exit/stop and test first/middle/final-
          owner cancellation. Cover exact/tag/family/mixed invalidation, stable
          deduplication, whole-batch validation, one revision, interruption, surviving
          subscriptions and zero-match no-op. Reject wildcard, zero-argument clear and
          ordinary cache-clear paths. Validate targets, stable expansion and
          deduplication, plus the 256-identity bound. Check missing/zero-match no-op,
          one revision, active-work fencing and no direct lookup.
        - Prove transition-owned finite work, atomic action batches, transaction
          reject/cancel/allow/serialize policies, explicit writes, cancellation
          boundaries, durable identity, reconciliation and compensation.
          Check stream emission, replacement, release and finalization. Hydration
          retains latest hasValue/value/count/generation/terminal state without replay;
          live input rematerializes streams.
          Use explicit `after` refresh events, one exact-key refresh and scheduling
          after settlement. Check suspend/dispose fencing; reject retry and poll APIs.
          Check Cause classification, stable diagnostics, typed unions, retained and
          post-boundary values, cross-actor reads, duplicate-stream rejection and
          bounded target expansion. @V5 owns seed validation, capability provenance
          and revocation, StoreKernel fencing/fanout, and absence of occurrence/public
          writers.

        The @V3 operation illustration is:
        ```ts
        key(P);
        getState(K);
        getData(K);
        setData;
        ```

        Check: owner-focused tests remain to be named for each mechanism. The pinned
        Node codec suite uses full boundary vectors; other hosts use C4's compact
        fixture. See @plan.Runtime.O2 for identity ownership.
    V4  Stories, commands, evidence and models
        Establishes @O2 and @O3. This Plan owns Story and evidence requirements;
        TESTING.md supplies source trace IDs and migration evidence.

        **Construction and ownership**
        - Expose only `story.app(runtimeSetup, options?)`,
          `story.machine(machine, options?)` and `story.actor(machine, options?)`.
          Keep option objects closed. App/machine options include fixtures,
          maxTurns, title, description and tags. Machine options add exact input and
          selected context; actor recipes use input and contextBindings. Default
          maxTurns to 100. Metadata does not affect identity.
        - Build fixture tuples with complete typed Implementations and optional
          Runtime-owned resource seeds. Require tuples to close external requirements;
          acquisition may fail through its typed error. Construct each fixture once per Runtime.
          A fixture overrides App Implementation for the same service; reject
          duplicate fixture providers. Give each run fresh state, supplied
          RuntimeSetup, deterministic TestClock host with fixture capabilities and
          isolated in-memory Persistence. Each App run calls `construct()` once.
        - Keep builders inert and persistent. `run()` alone executes and captures
          AppPlan, RuntimeSetup, Implementation, Persistence, Clock, fixtures, seeds
          and runner options. Exclude assertions, branches, loops, predicates,
          arbitrary callbacks, live handles and Runtime from plans. Each command
          returns a new plan and preserves its prior prefix. Reject bare Apps, live
          Runtime inputs and void-machine input. Reject context on context-free
          machines, missing fixtures, extra fields, boot unions, inherited Persistence,
          seed-based service satisfaction, matchers, control registries and public
          testing runtimes.
        - Keep recipes to exact machine, input and contextBindings. Recipe identity
          controls actor reuse; distinct recipes create separate actors. App sends
          target an exact recipe or app-owned ActorRef resolved through runtime lookup.
          Require exact binding keys, compatible/admitted refs and machines, repeated
          keys for one provider and transitive providers. Validate before construction;
          materialize providers first; retain leases; dispose Story leases in reverse
          dependency order. Reject runtime/mailbox/snapshot/state/operation/disposal
          fields, cycles, missing providers, foreign/mismatched/disposed refs, machine
          family targets and recipe use in React/runtime APIs. App-owned actors remain
          Runtime-owned after Story cleanup.
        - Compile focused machine Stories through a package-private AppPlan. Give
          each one fresh actor, target-free send, exact selected context and silent
          baseline. Route changed/equal setContext through production turns. Reject
          boot payloads, refs, extra actors, raw memory, initial state, snapshot
          overrides, direct mutation, synthetic events and App Story context injection.

        **Commands and cleanup**
        - Support `process`, `advance`, `advanceTo`, `advanceToNextTimer`,
          `checkpoint`, `run`, app `send(target, event)`, machine `send(event)` and
          machine `setContext(context)`. Use the exact production mailbox and its
          same-command private acknowledgement; do not drain unrelated work.
        - Let `process` drain ready mailbox, context, reconciliation, same-time
          deadlines, scheduler and finite fibers until a command or future time is
          needed. Leave pending external calls, streams and future deadlines visible.
          Do not advance time or invent results. Fail when unknown finite work reaches
          `maxTurns`. Clock movement never processes implicitly. Checkpoints capture
          immediately.
        - Give every run a fresh production Runtime and owned cancellation/finalization.
          Reject perform/deliver/receive/simulate/flush/settle/setTime/final/replay,
          result injection, pending-occurrence interception, operation registries,
          public persistence facts and replay of restored work. Hydrated streams start
          a new generation without replay.
        - Close admission and finalize non-abortably. Retain terminal lifecycle
          evidence and drain accepted evidence. Close sinks/queues; dispose Story
          leases in reverse order, then Runtime owners. Finalize fixture/host scopes
          last. Run every phase and order failures deterministically. Preserve
          execution/cancellation failure as primary; otherwise preserve the first
          cleanup failure.
          Either failure rejects the run.
        - Require one `FlowStoryExecutionError` envelope with checkpoints, optional
          end, failure boundary, primary and ordered cleanup diagnostics, cancellation,
          accepted/drained evidence sequence and complete Effect Cause.

        **Evidence capture**
        - Capture exact static Story closure in one atomic read cut. Include the
          machine actor, every recipe and exact ActorRefs in bindings, targets and
          transitive providers. Capture runtime time and pending work, actor issues,
          StoreState revision, published snapshots and accepted evidence prefix.
        - Capture `run.end` after commands and before cleanup. It does not imply actor
          completion. Failures retain completed checkpoints and truthful facts; never
          manufacture end. Capture does no live lookup, unrelated work, time movement,
          actor creation/disposal/restoration or external work.
        - Keep evidence non-resumable. Expose no fork, restore, fromCheckpoint or
          public dynamic getter. Resolve exact app recipes or app-owned refs. Reject
          unknown/inherited internal names by own-key check and existing
          FlowUsageError. Keep machine snapshots direct; add no issue/resource registry.

        **Models and host checks**
        - Accept pure models only from fresh command-empty machine Stories. App
          Stories prove cross-actor orchestration. Key every guard/redirect-relevant
          state, memory and seed fact; @V1 validates the canonical key. Use exact
          `{ events, maxDepth?, limit? }` options and call-owned events. Default
          maxDepth to 20 and require a non-negative safe integer. Default limit to
          100 and require a positive safe integer. Event cost is one. Expose only
          shortest/simple path discovery.
        - Keep traversal pure. Shortest paths use stable-order BFS; simple paths use
          stable-order DFS. Retain the zero-step path and first path per unseen key;
          repeat no key in one simple path. Return paths/truncated/explored. Set
          truncated when either bound hides a candidate. Fail synchronously on defects
          without partial results. Use no object/state-token
          fallback, Effects, services, kernels, fixtures, Runtime, mutation, inferred
          events, filters, selectors, weights, registries or synthetic routes.
        - Retain each path's predicted projection, per-step data/issues, weight,
          description and traversal metadata. `path.story` extends the base Story;
          `run()` proves live behavior and `process().run()` marks a ready boundary.
          Compare predicted final/prefix keys with live results. Model async outcomes
          only when events were authored as candidates. Predict no primitive snapshots
          and build no second interpreter/evidence shape. React remains outside the
          machine timeline.
        - Let host runners own assertions, pass/fail, retries and naming. Matchers
          run after `run()` or inspect FlowStoryExecutionError after rejection. Emit
          one TurnRecord per atomic machine-turn snapshot. Share LifecycleRecords
          through the sequenced hub/barrier without a machine turn or second history.
          Explicit buffers default to 256, accept non-negative capacity and return
          records plus `truncatedBeforeSequence`. Persistence stores no buffers;
          actors own no `setRetention` or mutable TraceLog.

        - TESTING's historical closure note points to UNRESOLVED_BEHAVIOR as history.
          Active requirements close focused-context, operation, occurrence,
          checkpoint and cleanup gaps. Post-bootstrap recipes use the shared atomic
          path and add no Story surface.
        - Deleted Story/scenario and child Story/model shapes belong to
          @plan.Cutover.V3 and @plan.Cutover.O2.

        The normative TESTING construction example is:
        ```ts
        const storyRun = story
          .app(runtimeSetup, {
            fixtures: [documentsFixture],
            maxTurns: 100,
            tags: ["smoke"],
          })
          .send(editor, Editor.E.EditRequested("Updated"))
          .process()
          .checkpoint("saved")
          .run();

        const focused = story.machine(editorMachine, {
          input: { documentId: "document-1" },
          context: { sessionState: Session.S.ACTIVE, themeMode: "dark" },
          fixtures: [editorFixture],
        });

        const local = story.actor(editorMachine, {
          input: { documentId: "document-1" },
          contextBindings: { sessionState: sessionRecipe },
        });
        ```
        The normative command example is:
        ```ts
        process(); advance(duration); advanceTo(epochMilliseconds);
        advanceToNextTimer(); checkpoint(name); run({ signal? });
        send(target, event); // App Story
        send(event); setContext(context); // Machine Story
        ```
        TESTING's focused-context example is:
        ```ts
        const signedOutStory = story
          .machine(editorMachine, {
            input: { documentId: "document-1" },
            context: {
              sessionState: Session.S.ACTIVE,
              themeMode: "dark",
            },
            fixtures: [editorFixture],
            maxTurns: 100,
          })
          .send(Editor.E.EditRequested())
          .setContext({
            sessionState: Session.S.SIGNED_OUT,
            themeMode: "dark",
          })
          .process()
          .checkpoint("signed-out");
        ```
        The normative evidence shape is:
        ```ts
        appRun.checkpoints["signed-out"].actor(editor).snapshot;
        appRun.checkpoints["signed-out"].actor(PrimarySessionRef).snapshot;
        appRun.checkpoints["signed-out"].runtime.now;
        appRun.checkpoints["signed-out"].runtime.pendingWork;

        appRun.end.actor(editor).snapshot;
        appRun.end.actor(PrimarySessionRef).snapshot;

        machineRun.checkpoints["saved"].snapshot;
        machineRun.checkpoints["saved"].runtime.now;
        machineRun.end.snapshot;
        machineRun.end.runtime.pendingWork;
        ```
        The compact production-Story proof illustration binds this surface to its
        result shapes:
        ```ts
        story.app(runtimeSetup, options?)
        process
        advanceTo
        checkpoint.actor(...)
        run.end
        ```
        **Unaccepted CONTRACT proposal value inventory**
        - `story` lists the three builders. Builder calls are inert; `run()` alone
          executes a plan.
        - `fixture` supplies complete services and optional Runtime-owned resource
          seeds. Fixtures are fresh per run; seeds satisfy no service requirement;
          duplicate providers reject.
        - `behavior` describes an inert gateway over one App and a non-empty Story-ID
          record. Validation is synchronous; the CLI is its only public consumer; it
          creates no Implementation, Runtime, actor, sink, execution or cleanup.
        - `model` describes pure structural traversal from a fresh, command-empty
          machine Story. `stateKey` returns canonical-key input; only shortest and
          simple path discovery are exposed. Search runs no Effects, services,
          fixtures or operations and does not mutate Runtime or synthesize outcomes.
        - `FlowStoryExecutionError` is the run/cancellation/cleanup rejection
          envelope. The proposal leaves its complete class signature and constructor
          unspecified. The envelope carries complete Cause, checkpoints, optional end,
          failure boundary, cleanup diagnostics, cancellation and accepted/drained
          evidence.

        Other §8 examples cover commands, checkpoint-name aliases, common-prefix
        branch plans and exact checkpoint keys/errors. They also show focused
        context, evidence reads, defensive lookup, gateway validation and live model
        paths. All §8 examples and value descriptions are unaccepted illustrations.
        Helper aliases are not exports; infer no unspecified signature.
        The proposal-only checkpoint notation includes:
        ```ts
        type NewCheckpointName<Names extends string, Name extends string> = string extends Name
          ? never
          : Extract<Name, Names> extends never
            ? Name
            : never;

        interface StoryPlan<Names extends string = never> {
          checkpoint<const Name extends string>(
            name: NewCheckpointName<Names, Name>,
          ): StoryPlan<Names | Name>;
          run(options?: { signal?: AbortSignal }): Promise<StoryRun<Names>>;
        }

        type StoryRun<Names extends string> = {
          checkpoints: {
            [Name in Names]: CheckpointEvidence;
          };
          end: EndEvidence;
        };
        ```
        These helper names illustrate checkpoint typing; they are not proposed exports.
        The separate persistent-branch illustration is:
        ```ts
        const common = story
          .machine(editorMachine, options)
          .send(Editor.E.Opened())
          .process()
          .checkpoint("opened");
 
        const saved = common.send(Editor.E.SaveRequested()).process().checkpoint("saved");
        const discarded = common.send(Editor.E.Discarded()).checkpoint("discarded");
        const cancelled = common.send(Editor.E.Cancelled()).checkpoint("cancelled");
        const savedRun = await saved.run();
        const discardedRun = await discarded.run();
        savedRun.checkpoints.opened; // valid
        savedRun.checkpoints.saved; // valid
        savedRun.checkpoints.typo; // TypeScript error
        // @ts-expect-error duplicate literal checkpoint name
        common.checkpoint("opened"); // duplicate literal: TypeScript error
        declare const runtimeName: string;
        // @ts-expect-error widened string cannot erase the known-name map
        common.checkpoint(runtimeName); // widened string: TypeScript error
        ```
        This shows one unchanged prefix and separate runs with exact names.
        The proposal's package-private defensive lookup illustration is:
        ```ts
        declare function lookupCheckpointInternal<Names extends string>(
          run: StoryRun<Names>,
          name: string,
        ): CheckpointEvidence;
        declare const untrustedCheckpointName: string;
        lookupCheckpointInternal(savedRun, untrustedCheckpointName); // unknown own key throws existing FlowUsageError
        ```
        The helper remains nonpublic. It rejects unknown own keys with existing
        FlowUsageError semantics.

        The proposal-only gateway and model examples are:
        ```ts
        const gateway = behavior({
          app: IncidentApp,
          stories: { smoke: incidentStory, "machine-model": incidentMachineStory },
        });

        const baseStory = story.machine(incidentMachine, { fixtures: [incidentApiFixture] });
        const incidentModel = model(baseStory, {
          stateKey: ({ value, memory }) => [value.id, memory],
        });
        const result = incidentModel.getShortestPaths({
          events: [NewIntent.E.IntentOpened("incident-1"), NewIntent.E.SessionEnded()],
          maxDepth: 20,
          limit: 100,
        });
        await result.paths[0]!.story.run();
        ```
        The gateway uses external IDs smoke and machine-model. It rejects empty or
        duplicate IDs, missing or mixed app identity, recipes, incompatible machine
        Stories, and foreign machines; it never executes. The model starts from a
        fresh machine Story and keys state with value.id plus memory. It traverses
        candidate domain events with bounds, then runs a returned path as live proof.
        These proposal examples add no accepted signatures or proof receipt.

        Check: focused TESTING owner proofs plus production Story/model tests.
        Named executable proof paths remain pending where implementation work has
        not supplied them; use focused checks in V7, not a second Story runtime.
    V5  React hosts and inspection evidence
        Establishes @O2.

        **React attachment and passive views**
        - Prove stable inert refs, exact wire identity and hook ownership. Hooks do
          not create, dispose or recover ownership. `useActor` owns one local actor;
          `useActorByRef` only looks up; `useView(actor, selector)` is the sole
          ordinary reactive subscription.
        - Prove render prepares one final actor/ref/snapshot/handle/mailbox, commit
          attaches it and imperative `createActor` attaches immediately. Cover
          provisional context recheck, a 64-command buffer with private bound and
          suspension retention/release/resume without replay. Cover one context
          reconciliation, Object.is/named-record suppression and optional `useShallow`.
        - Check one tear-free actor/store boundary, lifecycle ordering, FIFO
          settlement, truthful failures and late-completion fencing. Reject tuple
          changes without keyed remount, changed refs and abandoned/overflow mutation.
          Also reject suspended commands, partial actors on selector defects,
          comparator arguments, selector work, prepare evidence, overlapping
          generations and foreign/tombstoned providers.
        - Record exact descriptor/K reads per evaluation. Replace dependencies after
          evaluation. Matching StoreFanout reruns at one tear-free boundary; selection
          performs no work or mutation.

        **Lifecycle evidence**
        - Publish lifecycle snapshots before inspection events through the existing
          handle. Lifecycle is not a machine turn. Cover start/restore/suspend/
          resume/dispose, private publication/turn counters, hydration restoration,
          cause tags and one publication barrier/global sequence.
        - Check causal asynchronous evidence release, per-sink truncation/failure
          isolation, accepted-prefix drain, sequence exhaustion and disposal drain.
          Add no prepare event, synthetic TurnRecord or second mutable history.
          Live and Story runs agree on TurnRecord/LifecycleRecord facts.

        The compact React ownership illustration is:
        ```ts
        useActor;
        useActorByRef;
        useView(actor, selector);
        ```

        Check: packed React 18/19 consumers and application browser/lifecycle tests
        for reachability and host-specific behavior. Test paths and receipts are
        pending until assigned. The useView semantic owner is @plan.Runtime.O5.
    V6  Persistence, artifacts, server and CLI
        Establishes @O2.

        - Restore and validate before activation or handle escape. Install the
          baseline silently; order projections by dependency without replaying
          onContext. False/null produces no event. Close context cuts with exact
          refs/revisions. Opaque providers report NonDurableContextProvider.
          Hydrated streams expose latest/terminal state without replay and require
          live input to rematerialize. Cover trusted seeding, WebStorage, IndexedDB
          and persistence codecs.
        - Test storage errors, malformed data, identity mismatch, default/custom
          codecs and ConcurrentDehydrate. Retain the last good record. Custom-codec
          failure commits no Runtime mutation.
        - Check app Story/actor lookup/run.end, module ownership, compound state,
          context, lifecycle, operation identity, bounds, sinks and formatting.
          Reject old root/child/final/replay/Story shapes. Reject invalid or duplicate
          IDs/machines/module ownership, missing refs, bad defaults and unresolved
          requirements. Reject invalid Story metadata, malformed traces, impossible
          lifecycle tuples and inconsistent evidence.
        - Reject missing, duplicate, overlapping or structural redaction paths.
          Do not mutate raw, Runtime or persisted truth. Share artifacts cannot be
          CLI/import/replay/Persistence/boot/evidence authority inputs. Invalid
          vectors report code/category/path/bound; EvidenceUnavailable has a
          non-null truncation marker. Keep raw diagnostics ordered and byte-stable;
          keep share exports deterministic and privacy-reduced.
        - Check CLI grammar, gateway containment, execution, formatting, files,
          signals/exits and Story/CLI parity. Keep one Diagnostic/result owner,
          bounded round-trip, ordered diagnostics, in-process Cause preservation,
          atomic publication and legacy-shape rejection.

        Native mapping for the artifact/CLI proof illustration:
        | Evidence surface | Native owner |
        | --- | --- |
        | Raw artifact representation | @V6 |
        | Redacted export | @V6 |
        | CLI integration | @V6 |
        | CLI cutover absence | @plan.Cutover.V5 |
        The unaccepted proposal's §12 provenance points persistence/artifacts to
        the v2 wire contracts, public export and migration amendment. It transfers
        no authority. Check production codec/storage/artifact/inspect/CLI tests and
        redacted-export tests. Exact paths remain pending when not named.
        Full codec vectors run on pinned Node; compact host receipts use @V3.
        CONTRACT §11 remains an unchecked proposal checklist until proofs and gates
        run. No checkbox is a receipt.
    V7  Crosswalk, proving applications and gate sequence
        Establishes @O1, @O2 and @O4.

        **Proof ownership and examples**
        - Route, declaration and composition proofs belong to @V1. Runtime/mailbox
          proofs belong to @V2; operations to @V3; Stories to @V4; React/inspection
          to @V5; artifacts/CLI to @V6. Cutover checks belong to
          @plan.Cutover.V1 through @plan.Cutover.V7.
        - Keep proof-matrix examples with their owners: type/application identity
          @V1; actor ownership @V2; operation calls @V3; Story execution @V4.
          Hooks belong to @V5; artifact and CLI evidence to @V6; focused commands
          to @V7. Examples add no owner or receipt.

        **Proving applications**
        - Todo Essentials covers definitions, compound states, refs/leases,
          operations, actions, views, React, fixtures, Stories, checkpoints and
          TestClock.
        - Incident Console covers refs/bindings, transaction conflicts, streams,
          leases, inspection, diagnostics, CLI/artifacts and browser lifetime.
          Hydrated Offline Notes covers request isolation, context-closed
          persistence, first-render consistency, outbox/reconnect/disposal, exact
          hydration and host-seeding behavior.
        - Include bounded-feed application-authored dropping/coalescing streams,
          React 18/19 and isolated TypeScript packages. Reject Flow pressure options,
          inferred app requirements and showcase-only React/type packages.
        - @plan.Cutover.V3 owns deletion/retention rows, exports/declarations,
          packed consumers, docs, dependency hygiene, generated output and
          compatibility absence. Detailed ownership belongs to @plan.Cutover.O3.

        **Focused greenfield boundary**
        ```sh
        nubx tsc -p packages/flow-state-rewrite/tsconfig.json --noEmit
        vp test packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/<focused-proof>.test.ts
        vp lint packages/flow-state-rewrite/src
        ```
        Frozen `packages/flow-state` gates do not prove replacement. After rewrite
        entrypoints exist, run package-local type, test, lint, build and packed
        consumer checks. Published-package, browser and workspace checks remain late.

        **Current frozen-package gates and rewrite gates**
        Gates 2–5 below are source migration checks for frozen packages/flow-state.
        Their results do not prove replacement conformance. Run rewrite gates against
        packages/flow-state-rewrite:
        - Types: `nub run --filter flow-state-rewrite check:types`.
        - Focused tests: `vp test packages/flow-state-rewrite/src`.
        - Lint: `vp lint packages/flow-state-rewrite/src`.
        - Build and packed consumers: `nub run --filter flow-state-rewrite build` and
          `nub run --filter flow-state-rewrite check:packed-consumers`.
        - Static harness: `nub run --filter flow-state-rewrite check:static-harness`.
        The root `nub run verify` still builds the frozen package through `build`; it is
        not replacement closure until root build/docs and late gates target the rewrite.

        Gates 1–4:
        1. Focused proof files for affected owner invariants.
        2. CLI-source types: `nub run --filter flow-state check:cli-source-types`.
        3. Package tests: `nub run --filter flow-state test`.
        4. Package build: `nub run --filter flow-state build`.
        Gates 5–8:
        5. Packed consumers for public/declaration changes:
           `nub run --filter flow-state check:packed-consumers`.
        6. Affected consumer tests and builds.
        7. App browser tests for browser/lifecycle changes.
        8. `nub run verify` for workspace closure after replacement-specific gates.

        The source commands use `flow-state` and script owners
        `packages/flow-state/package.json` and `root package.json`. The package-local
        rewrite gates above use `flow-state-rewrite`. Browser, published-package, docs,
        and final workspace gates remain late cutover obligations; preserve their
        current targets until product cutover updates their script owners.

        CONTRACT.md §11 remains an unaccepted checklist. Keep every item unchecked
        until its executable proof and gates run. This native owner map is not a
        receipt:
        | Checklist area | Native owner |
        | --- | --- |
        | Exact public types and inference | @V1 |
        | Inert AppPlan and closed admission | @V1 |
        | Mailbox, turns, publication and context | @V2 |
        | Runtime, leases, lifecycle and cleanup | @V2 |
        | Disposal leases, dependent blocking and tombstones | @V2; @plan.Cutover.V3 |
        | Descriptor identity, bytes, generations, CAS and seeds | @V3 |
        | Store ownership, passive reads and invalidation | @V3 |
        | Actions, transactions, streams and Cause projection | @V3 |
        | Story builders, fixtures, recipes and cleanup | @V4 |
        | Commands, TestClock, prefix branching and isolation | @V4 |
        | Atomic checkpoint/end evidence and lookup absence | @V4; @V6 |
        | Pure model traversal and live parity | @V4 |
        | React refs, hooks, hosts and passive views | @V5 |
        | Lifecycle inspection, sequencing and sinks | @V5 |
        | Persistence, artifacts, server, inspection and CLI | @V6 |
        | Proving applications and bounded-feed evidence | @V7 |
        | Cutover disposition and gates | @plan.Cutover.V3–V7 |
        Its §12 provenance is also unaccepted:
        - Routes map to PUBLIC_API and @V1/@V6; types map to PUBLIC_API, TYPE_SYSTEM
          and @V1; operations map to PUBLIC_API, TYPE_SYSTEM, SEMANTICS and @V3.
        - Modules/refs map to PUBLIC_API, ARCHITECTURE, REACT_AND_HOSTS and
          @V1/@V2/@V5; Runtime maps to TYPE_SYSTEM, ARCHITECTURE and @V2; React maps
          to REACT_AND_HOSTS and @V5.
        - Persistence/artifacts map to their wire contract and @V6. Testing/Stories
          map to PUBLIC_API, TYPE_SYSTEM, TESTING and @V4. Inspection maps to
          PUBLIC_API, TESTING and @V5/@V6; CLI maps to PUBLIC_API, CLI and @V6.
        - Acceptance maps to @V1 through @V7 and @plan.Cutover.V3 through
          @plan.Cutover.V7. Source paths are provenance; Workflow Plans own semantics.

        Check: focused commands above and exact package/consumer/browser gates as
        their owners become available. Final `nub run verify` runs only at
        closure. Actual run results belong in supported Task records, not Plan
        status. No passed receipt is recorded here.
    V8  Implementation review and promotion
        Establishes @O1 and @O4. For each slice, the reviewer handback names exact
        contract IDs, changed files, focused checks, lint pass/fail, behavior
        reviewed, fixed/blocking/advisory findings. No unresolved lint, proof gap
        or blocking finding advances. Review both allowed behavior and negative
        boundaries; check one semantic owner, no hidden compatibility, uncontrolled
        work, partial updates, or implementation-detail-only tests.

        A slice becomes ready for dependents only after focused checks pass and
        named owner proof is recorded. Complete replacement requires public
        behavior/types, failure lanes, cleanup, persistence, artifacts, CLI, React,
        Stories, packed consumers, browsers and deletion proofs. Use @V1 through
        @V7, @plan.Cutover.V3 and final workspace gates. Check links are V1-V7
        and their named commands; unnamed owner tests are explicitly pending.
        No individual green test, typecheck, lint or source scan closes cutover.

questions
    Q1  Root gates after package promotion
        The package-local rewrite gates are mapped in @V7. Root build and docs gates,
        published-package checks, and browser gates still need product cutover owners
        and target updates. Keep frozen-package results as migration evidence; do not
        treat them as rewrite proof or record cutover until those gates pass.

